Fillets: a walked blend whose contact reaches the rounded rim of a hole or
cut-out of its face, rounded more sharply than the ball, runs on over the
gap on that face's surface and the rim cuts it (mitcad#238), as an
analytic blend over a hole does (0055, 0071): e.g. the edge of a block
rounded with 3 past a hole whose rim is rounded with 0.5, the contact on
the top crossing the hole's mouth.

- ChFi3d_Builder::StartSol: where a contact reaches an edge into a face
  that continues its face tangentially, the walk went on onto that face.
  A face that bends towards the ball more sharply than the ball there
  (ballCannotTouch: a principal curvature towards the ball's centre above
  1/R, e.g. a convex rounding of a smaller radius under a ball inside the
  solid, a concave one under a ball outside it) continues nothing: that
  side has no neighbouring face. Before, the walk's first step on it left
  it at once (CallPerformSurf : Path failed) or the walk ran on another
  branch of the solution. At an end of the spine the blend then rolls on
  past the face's boundary on the face's own surface (0076).
- PerformSetOfSurfOnElSpine: where one side has no neighbouring face
  inside the spine (before: Chaining is impossible),
  ChFi3d_Builder::WalkedBridgeOverGap (new) walks the blend on from the
  last piece's end section over that face's surface made unbounded, the
  other face as it is, until the contact comes back into the face across
  another edge of the rim, or up to where the next piece starts (on a
  closed spine, the stripe's first). Each stretch walked is cut by the
  rim (ChFi3d_KPartCutPiece): its side on the face becomes the blend's
  intersection with the rim. A stretch also ends where the other contact
  reaches an edge of its face (a seam, a tangent face); the walk goes on
  from there as StartSol sets it up. Where the cut crosses the rim's seam
  the stretch is two pieces, each ending on the seam's p-curve on its
  side, the seam kept on the side away from the spine (its end under the
  blend goes); where the cut is two lines of the surfaces' intersection
  that meet, two pieces cut up to where they meet
  (ChFi3d_KPartCutJunction). The common points the cut makes get the
  tangents of their contact lines, as the walk gives its own. Not done
  where the face is not a face of the spine's edges (a start that
  overflowed onto a face beyond: round a closed spine the face it
  consumes is not cut loose), where the rim is not tangent to the face,
  or where the contact comes back across an edge of another face.
- ChFi3d_KPartCutCurve: of the arcs of the surfaces' intersection across
  the blend between the cut's ends, an arc on the face beyond is taken
  first (a rounded rim whose surface the blend cuts twice; one of the two
  arcs lies on the rim's face), then any as before.
